          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"dumas-fils-la-dame-aux-camelias-8395d5328906e125\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":2,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Marguerite’s “je t’aime” is primarily an explicit avowal of her loving state. It also moderately supports performative force because, in this exchange, it ratifies her choice of Armand and accompanies a concrete undertaking to abandon her former life. The exclamation, murmuring, embrace, and emotion intensify the avowal but do not independently show that the words are a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The main uncertainty is the degree of P: the surrounding turn strongly commits Marguerite to a shared life, but much of that undertaking is expressed by the following future-tense promises rather than by “je t’aime” alone.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Marguerite directly addresses Armand in dialogue quoted by the retrospective first-person narrator; the highlighted phrase is the first “je t’aime” in her turn.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Marguerite answers Armand’s declaration of mutual devotion after he overhears her refusing the duke’s financial support rather than leave him. Her avowal is corroborated by that costly private choice and by her subsequent conduct. In the same turn, she projects a peaceful shared future and vows to leave her former life, giving the exchange some commitment-enacting force. Her murmuring, embrace, and emotional state mark intense delivery but do not establish independent E force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Oh! oui, je t’aime, mon Armand! ... je t’aime comme je n’aurais pas cru pouvoir aimer.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person avowal and comparison to her previously imagined capacity for love strongly support T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand, Marguerite refuses to leave Armand despite the duke’s threat to withdraw financial support: “je ne quitterai pas Armand ... C’est peut-être une folie, mais je l’aime!”\",\"supports\":\"Her costly declaration to Prudence, made before Armand enters, corroborates the avowal’s contextual sincerity and her relational choice.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"In the same turn she says, “Nous serons heureux, nous vivrons tranquilles, et je dirai un éternel adieu à cette vie.”\",\"supports\":\"The avowal participates in a broader undertaking toward a shared life, providing moderate support for P.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"She “murmura” the words while putting both arms around Armand’s neck; the scene also describes tears and an emotional voice.\",\"supports\":\"These details establish affectionate, intense delivery but, without evidence of blurting or involuntary verbal discharge, do not independently support E.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator says that afterward she broke with former friends and habits, gave Armand the duke’s letters unread, and showed him sustained love and care.\",\"supports\":\"Subsequent conduct reinforces the passage’s presentation of the avowal as genuine and connected to an enacted relational commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev6\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-tense explicit love utterance in Chapter XVII of Alexandre Dumas fils’s La Dame aux camélias.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the linguistic form and bibliographic location of the classified occurrence.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the dominant avowal, while a moderate P score captures its role in ratifying a consequential relational choice. Emotional delivery can remain contextual without requiring E, and no important core-force residue requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The classification distinguishes Marguerite’s first highlighted “je t’aime” from the repetition later in the same turn, while using the complete turn as contextual evidence.\"}"
        }
